   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the general business law, in relation to the applicability of\nthe security guard act of nineteen hundred ninety-two to facilities owned by\npublic entities",
    "basePrintNo": "S7142",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S7142-2021",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [],
     "size": 0
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Amd §89-w, Gen Bus L",
    "lawSection": "General Business Law",
    "memo": "\n \nBILL NUMBER: S7142\n\nSPONSOR: KENNEDY\n \nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to amend the general business law, in relation to the applicabil-\nity of the security guard act of nineteen hundred ninety-two to facili-\nties owned by public entities\n\n \nPURPOSE OR GENERAL IDEA OF BILL:\n\nTo extend the exemption from the Security Guard Act for public entities\nthat hire security guards on a temporary basis for less than 15 days\neach year to the tenants of facilities owned by public entities.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nSection 89-w of the General Business Law is amended to extend the\nexemption from the Security Guard Act for public entities that hire\nsecurity guards on a temporary basis for less than 15 days each year to\nthe tenants of facilities owned by public entities. The current law\nprovides an exemption for public entities that hire security guards on a\ntemporary basis, defined as less than 15 days each year.  This bill\nwould extend the existing exemption to the tenants of facilities owned\nby public entities.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nThe exemption to the Security Guard Law for the temporary hiring of\nsecurity guards for less than 15 days by a public entity recognized the\nchallenge hiring licensed security guards on a limited basis and the\nexpectation that a public entity would adhere to appropriate security\nprotocols without a licensing requirement for their staff.  In the many\nyears since this exemption was originally enacted many public entities\nhave chosen to outsource the management of their facilities and with it\nthe responsibilities for hiring security.  Extending the public entity\nexemption to tenants of public entities will continue to permit these\nsame short term uses under the same conditions as the original\nexemptions.\n\n \nPRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\nThis is a new bill.\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS FOR STATE AND LOCAL GOVERNMENTS:\n\nNone.\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nImmediately.\n",
